    {
        "content": "<p>Can you see which positivity extension is taking so long? (I don't remember if positivity's built-in logs are good enough. But that can be changed!)</p>",
        "id": 612184528,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784739872
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>looks like it's searching the local context for inequalities to use</p>",
        "id": 612191595,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1784741663
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is this <code>positivity</code> call taking place inside a <code>def</code> by chance? It looks like it is from the local context and the huge number of type variables, each of which has a bunch of instances. Sometimes it can be useful to section variables more carefully to avoid issues like this. Of course, that's not to say we shouldn't fix the issue with <code>positivity</code> here.</p>",
        "id": 612195064,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1784742549
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>it looks like most of those typeclass variables appear in the goal though</p>",
        "id": 612196415,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1784742940
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/9b53612f8d8de79c06e752024f9b087e9ea5d0fa/Mathlib/Analysis/Distribution/SchwartzSpace/Basic.lean#L692\">this</a> is this slow <code>positivity</code> call?</p>",
        "id": 612197006,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1784743112
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>To me, all of those <code>isDefEq</code> calls look like they might be <code>~q(...)</code> matches.</p>\n<p>Note that in the implementation of <code>ring</code>, we do a match on the head constant before doing <code>~q(...)</code> avoid calling <code>isDefEq</code> on things that obviously don't unify. We might want to do the same here.</p>",
        "id": 612200053,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1784743945
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>my guess is they're coming from <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Mathlib.Meta.Positivity.compareHyp#doc\">docs#Mathlib.Meta.Positivity.compareHyp</a> being called <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/9b53612f8d8de79c06e752024f9b087e9ea5d0fa/Mathlib/Tactic/Positivity/Core.lean#L455-L457\">in a loop</a> on every hypothesis on the context</p>",
        "id": 612201669,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1784744438
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42014\">#42014</a> gets some speedup here: <code>-0.11%</code></p>",
        "id": 612207840,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1784746472
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Interesting that the radar output shows that precisely the file I highlighted is sped up by the most (-5.0G). The reason we were looking at it at all is that it's part of the longest pole and it's one of the slowest files to compile on the path (see <a href=\"https://speed.lean-lang.org/mathlib4-out/3d09388a78937307f9340f527afd7a91436e3cba/\">here</a>).</p>",
